## Final Judgement
Based on the fact-check record, the key finding is that while the total value of loans from the African Export-Import Bank (Afreximbank) was reported (e.g., US$15.20 billion as of mid-2020), the available evidence does not contain any data on the distribution of these loans by country. Multiple queries specifically about Zimbabwe's share, its comparison to other borrowers, and the basis for the claim's metric returned no relevant information from the retrieved sources.

The primary information lacking is any official or verified data on the portion of Afreximbank's total loans that was allocated to Zimbabwe. Without this specific figure, it is impossible to calculate Zimbabwe's share and compare it to the "more than half" threshold asserted in the claim.

Given that the fact-check retrieved no evidence to support the claim and the available evidence on total bank lending provides no insight into Zimbabwe's specific share, the claim cannot be verified. Therefore, based on the information gathered, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found no evidence to support the claim, as sources such as Afreximbank's [mid-2020 performance report](https://www.afreximbank.com/afreximbank-delivers-solid-performance-in-h1-2020/) and a [parliamentary transcript](https://newzwire.live/biti-vs-mthuli-transcript-finance-minister-goes-face-to-face-with-mps-on-economy/) provided total loan figures but no country-specific distribution data. Without verified statistics on Zimbabwe's share, the assertion that it constitutes more than half of the bank's loans could not be substantiated, leading to the claim being refuted.